The solution will be designed as a non-decisional support tool, ensuring that no clinical or medico-administrative decision is automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. Our AI models will provide explainable outputs to facilitate auditing a posteriori, with source citations, retrieved passages, and a retrieval trace for every interaction. Every interaction will be logged for posteriori audit, and human oversight will be built into the workflow.

We will deploy our Atelier platform on the Sovereign HDS tier of OuraneCloud, utilizing our sovereign European open-weight model, which meets the tender's requirements for data sovereignty and hosting on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure within the EU. Our API-first design will enable seamless integration with the client's existing hospital information system (SIH) and electronic patient record (DPI), supporting health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR) and the national health identifier (INS).

To ensure compliance with regulatory requirements, we have implemented several security measures, including data hosting on OuraneCloud, a SecNumCloud-qualified sovereign cloud; encryption using TLS 1.3 for data in transit and AES-256 for data at rest; pseudonymisation to protect sensitive data; and tenant isolation and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to safeguard sensitive information. We also adhere to the principle of data minimisation, ensuring that only necessary data is processed.

Our proposed solution will be delivered through a hybrid delivery model, combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure suited to public and health procurement. We will work closely with the client through a joint steering committee and an operational committee to ensure that our solution meets their requirements and expectations.

The project will be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter followed by progressive generalisation to all services. Change-management support and staff training will be provided to ensure a smooth transition.

We estimate that one-off project costs will be around 30% of the total budget, which is 270,000 euros, and will be incurred over an 18-month period. Recurring run costs will be around 70%, which is 630,000 euros, over 5 years. We target a gross margin between 25% and 35% and can propose cost-optimization strategies to achieve this while maintaining an acceptable margin.

Our solution has demonstrated efficiency outcomes in comparable deployments, such as reducing processing and documentation times, and we believe it can bring similar benefits to the hospital setting. By embedding our AI assistant into the staff's existing work environment and tools, we can avoid disrupting clinical or administrative workflows while augmenting them in place.

Finally, we are committed to minimizing the environmental footprint of our solution. Our partnership with green hosting providers such as OuraneCloud ensures that our solution is hosted on sovereign infrastructure in the EU region, utilizing renewable energy and having a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2. We also practice digital sobriety through model right-sizing, caching, and batching, contributing to reducing energy consumption. Our carbon reporting capability allows us to monitor and report the solution's carbon footprint, ensuring transparency and the ability to make data-driven decisions to minimize environmental impact.

In conclusion, Faber IA is confident that our proposed AI-powered medical documentation assistant can improve the efficiency of hospital staff at CHRB while maintaining the quality and safety of care delivered to patients. We believe that our solution meets the client's requirements and expectations, and we look forward to the opportunity to work with them to deliver a successful project.
